---
import {version as coreVersion} from '@krigsvold/theme';
import {themes} from '../../../src/palettes.mjs';
import {chatgptImport} from '../../../src/app-ports.mjs';
import {version} from '../../../package.json';
const family=themes.light.id.replace(/-light$/,''),org=family+'theme',name=themes.light.name.replace(/ Light$/,'');
const repo=(kind)=>`https://github.com/${org}/${kind}`;
---
<div class="app-ports" style={`--port-line:var(--${family}-line,${themes.light.tokens.line});--port-panel:var(--${family}-panel,${themes.light.tokens.panel})`}>
  <article aria-labelledby="npm-title"><h3 id="npm-title">For your next web project.</h3><p>CSS, JSON tokens, JavaScript exports, TypeScript declarations, and highlight.js styles. One package, both appearances.</p><p><code>@krigsvold/theme</code></p><p class="port-links"><a href={`/downloads/krigsvold-theme-${coreVersion}.tgz`} download>Download npm archive ↓</a><a href={repo('npm')}>npm source ↗</a></p><details><summary>Use the npm package</summary><p>Install the published package from npm:</p><pre><code>npm install @krigsvold/theme</code></pre><pre><code>{`import '@krigsvold/theme/css';
import { dark } from '@krigsvold/theme';
console.log(dark.tokens.accent);`}</code></pre><p>Set <code>data-{family}="light"</code> or <code>data-{family}="dark"</code> on the element to theme. Read colors using <code>var(--{family}-accent)</code>. CommonJS is supported. For highlight.js, import <code>@krigsvold/theme/highlight-dark.css</code> or its Light equivalent.</p></details></article>
  <article aria-labelledby="vim-title"><h3 id="vim-title">Vim &amp; Neovim.</h3><p>Syntax, editor surfaces, diffs, and diagnostics, with truecolor and 256-color support.</p><p class="port-links"><a href={`/downloads/${family}-vim.zip`} download>Download colorschemes ↓</a><a href={repo('vim')}>Vim source ↗</a></p><details><summary>Install the Vim colorscheme</summary><p>Extract the archive and copy the files in <code>colors/</code> into <code>~/.vim/colors/</code> for Vim or <code>~/.config/nvim/colors/</code> for Neovim. Then add:</p><pre><code>{`set termguicolors
set background=dark
colorscheme ${family}`}</code></pre><p>Set <code>background=light</code> for Light. Explicit names <code>{family}-light</code> and <code>{family}-dark</code> also work. The source repository supports native packages and plugin managers.</p></details></article>
  <article aria-labelledby="emacs-title"><h3 id="emacs-title">Emacs.</h3><p>Light and Dark themes for font-lock, Org, diffs, completion, and the editor interface.</p><p class="port-links"><a href={`/downloads/${family}-emacs.zip`} download>Download Emacs themes ↓</a><a href={repo('emacs')}>Emacs source ↗</a></p><details><summary>Install the Emacs theme</summary><p>Copy the two <code>-theme.el</code> files into <code>~/.emacs.d/themes/</code>. Add to your init file:</p><pre><code>{`(add-to-list 'custom-theme-load-path
             (expand-file-name "themes/" user-emacs-directory))
(load-theme '${family}-dark t)`}</code></pre><p>Use <code>{family}-light</code> for Light. Disable another active theme before switching. The final <code>t</code> skips the source approval prompt; use it after reviewing the files. Emacs 27.1 or newer. MELPA publication is pending.</p></details></article>
  <article aria-labelledby="browser-title"><h3 id="browser-title">A browser to match.</h3><p>Colors for tabs, toolbars, and new-tab surfaces. Firefox follows light/dark appearance; Chrome has separate variants.</p><p class="port-links"><a href={`/downloads/${family}-light-chrome.zip`} download>Chrome Light ↓</a><a href={`/downloads/${family}-dark-chrome.zip`} download>Chrome Dark ↓</a><a href={`/downloads/${family}-firefox.zip`} download>Firefox ↓</a></p><p class="port-links"><a href={repo('chrome')}>Chrome source ↗</a><a href={repo('firefox')}>Firefox source ↗</a></p><details><summary>Try the browser themes</summary><p>These are source packages prepared for marketplace submission. Store publication and Firefox signing are pending.</p><p>For Chrome, extract one archive. Open <code>chrome://extensions</code>, enable Developer mode, choose <strong>Load unpacked</strong>, and select the extracted folder containing <code>manifest.json</code>.</p><p>For Firefox, extract the archive. Open <code>about:debugging#/runtime/this-firefox</code>, choose <strong>Load Temporary Add-on</strong>, and select <code>manifest.json</code>. It lasts until Firefox restarts. Firefox 142 or newer.</p><p>These packages contain no scripts or browsing permissions. They color browser UI; website content keeps its own styling.</p></details></article>
  <article class="chatgpt-port" aria-labelledby="chatgpt-title"><h3 id="chatgpt-title">ChatGPT / Codex desktop.</h3><p>Copy a theme string and paste it into the desktop app’s theme import control in <strong>Settings → Appearance</strong>.</p><p class="port-links">{['light','dark'].map(mode=><button type="button" data-copy={chatgptImport(themes[mode])} data-copy-label={`${name} ${mode} theme import`} aria-label={`Copy ${name} ${mode} theme import`}>Copy {mode === 'light' ? 'Light' : 'Dark'} theme</button>)}</p><p class="port-links"><a href={`/downloads/${family}-light-chatgpt.txt`} download>Light import string ↓</a><a href={`/downloads/${family}-dark-chatgpt.txt`} download>Dark import string ↓</a></p><div class="preset-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${name} ChatGPT appearance values`}><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Light</th><th scope="col">Dark</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[['Background','bg'],['Foreground','text'],['Accent','accent']].map(([label,key])=><tr><th scope="row">{label}</th>{['light','dark'].map(mode=><td><button type="button" data-copy={themes[mode].tokens[key]} aria-label={`Copy ${name} ${mode} ChatGPT ${label.toLowerCase()} ${themes[mode].tokens[key]}`}>{themes[mode].tokens[key]}</button></td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p class="port-links"><a href={`/downloads/${family}-light-chatgpt.json`} download>Light settings ↓</a><a href={`/downloads/${family}-dark-chatgpt.json`} download>Dark settings ↓</a></p><p class="caption">Import strings use the codex-theme-v1 format from a desktop app export. They include diff and skill colors, default fonts, and opaque windows. Manual settings remain available above. Clients offering only preset accent colors cannot reproduce the full palette.</p></article>
</div>
